#{[17592186508855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508855 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508855 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508855 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508855 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e78a58b-7fed-5d4a-8336-150e88af3ff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508886 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1429b72-180d-5d2a-ae39-57cc2cf7b25c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-238044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+y=8 \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09338f11-6e50-5f7e-baa9-764c66288045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508913 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $-7.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $7,$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $7,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-7$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+y=8\\,)\\,\\times (7) \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-35x+7y=56 \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35x+\\cancel{7y} & =56 \\\\ 8x-\\cancel{7y} & =25 \\\\ (-35x+8x) & =(56+25) \\\\ -27x & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $7,$ and add the equations*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-238035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ 2x+5y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a68ca7c-dc16-5d70-b5c4-e7c98a96c8e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508917 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $10,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-10,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ (\\,2x+5y=-4\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ -10x-25y=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10x}-3y & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{10x}-25y & =20 \\\\ (-3y-25y) & =(36+20) \\\\ -28y & =56 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-5,$ and add the equations*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-238082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ 2x+6y=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4775db8b-54b5-507a-83b4-6fee15253323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508926 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the  first  equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ (\\,2x+6y=12\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ -6x-18y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{6x}+13y & =21 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}-18y & =-36 \\\\ (13y-18y) & =(21-36) \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+13y & =21 \\\\ 6x+13(3) & =21 \\\\ 6x+39 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $q.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c472f9d-cfda-5414-a840-ee6d18544bcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508927 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3p+2q=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p-4q=-22 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-6p-\\cancel{4q} & =-22 \\\\ 5p+\\cancel{4q} & =19 \\\\ (-6p+5p) & =(-22+19) \\\\ -p & =-3 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=3.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+2q & =11 \\\\ 3(3)+2q & =11 \\\\ 9+2q & =11 \\\\ 2q & =2 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-238051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ p+q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ba7c176-1d51-52ad-ad1b-a588df23024a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508932 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ (\\,p+q=4\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ -2p-2q=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3p+\\cancel{2q} & =11 \\\\ -2p-\\cancel{2q} & =-8 \\\\ (3p-2p) & =(11-8) \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-2,$ and add the equations*." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
